From: Andrey Stoyan <andrey.stoyan.csam@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 23 Aug 2026 15:05:29 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 34/44] fix(chexc): conformance after re-reading sections 3, 5
 and 6

Two rules were implemented more narrowly than the text states, and one diagnostic behaviour was
losing information:

- '@AssumeThrows' is rejected on a package that RECORDS capability lists of its own (6.3.2), and
  6.1 says a 'warn' package records trusted lists. The check only rejected packages verified at
  'error' -- a distinction the text does not make.
- The warning group is named 'checked-exceptions' (6.1), not 'chexc', which is the flag's alias.
- A requirement site missing several capabilities reported one of them. The compiler's diagnostic
  set deduplicates by position and severity alone, so the rest were dropped and appeared one per
  rebuild -- the loop a migration cannot afford. Misses are batched per site and reported
  together; a site missing exactly one keeps the singular message.

Probed and already conforming, recorded rather than changed: an entry whose type instantiates to
an unchecked exception vanishes (5.1.2), the join of functional types unions their capability
lists (3.4.3), and a public macro is never inferred (5.3.1).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
